  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Overview
    
    A succession crisis within the Aurora Stack's mystic hierarchy during the Clever Ice Age, triggered by the death of senior mystic Meridian-of-the-Seventh-House during a routine aurora-alignment ritual at The Dawn Terrace, an aurora-light repository on the Berg's surface. The crisis fractured the E'frostlings — Aurora Stack's dominant merchant-class faction — along fault lines that, by every indication in the deep ice, had been widening for decades before anyone acknowledged them.
    
    ## Sequence of Events
    
    Meridian died mid-invocation. Within hours, four family constellations claimed the vacant position through competing readings of ice-memory texts old enough that no authoritative original survives — I have looked. The younger mystics adopted crystal jewelry as faction markers. The E'frostlings splintered.
    
    Meridian's unfinished work on The Shell Hatch — a technique for amplifying aurora-signals across distance — became the crisis's true accelerant. Control of the Shell Hatch meant control of communication, and communication meant leverage. One faction threatened to sever fire-core shipments to compel compliance, which brought the Flipper Accord closer to collapse than any external threat had managed.
    
    The Tertiary Spire collapsed during the third formal succession challenge when two rival factions activated their crystalline arrays simultaneously. The resonance shattered the structure from within. Three candidates died in the collapse. The broader toll is recorded as seventeen — a figure I have not been able to verify against the ice-memory at that depth, though the names of Qingyaieifu (void-banisher), Shanfongqongja Teir'Gleam, and Ouchangzhaijun (thaw-survivor, Echo: Xe) were inscribed into later accord preambles as memorial.
    
    ## Aftermath
    
    The Nightshelf delegation that arrived for quarterly trade found Aurora Stack penguins standing in separate clusters, refusing mutual acknowledgment. The Nightshelf were, by all accounts, unsurprised. Their record-keeping from this period suggests they had anticipated the fracture well before it occurred.
    
    The crisis was ultimately contained — the Accord held — but the resolution produced the conditions that later necessitated the Accord of Beacon Light and Deep Fire between Holdborn (ice-thresh) and the Guild Weal' Clique~ of Nightfall Shelf. Containment, as that later accord's own language concedes, was understood as delay rather than cure.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
